  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson. <emphasis> </emphasis></para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_15b7b257-480b-4055-a9e9-6ce79260cafc" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>1. Xander is loading a pallet with boxes that each weighs 45 pounds. The pallet can safely support no more than 900 pounds. How many boxes can he safely load onto the pallet?____ boxes or less</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! 20</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.2. </para><para>The correct answer is 20. 
        Each box weighs 45 pounds. The total amount of weight the pallet can support is 900 pounds.
        Each box weighs 45 pounds. The total amount of weight the pallet can support is 900 pounds.
        Let \(b\) represent the number of boxes that can safely be loaded onto the pallet.
        Let \(b\) represent the number of boxes that can safely be loaded onto the pallet.
        The equation is : 45b≤900
        The equation is : 45b≤900
        Solve for \(b\) : b≤20
        Solve for \(b\) : b≤20
        The number of boxes that can be safely loaded onto the pallet is less than or equal to 20.
      
        The number of boxes that can be safely loaded onto the pallet is less than or equal to 20.
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_2fa05935-8dc2-4217-8896-72f9156be9f1" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>2. The elevator in Yehire’s apartment building has a sign that says the maximum weight is 2100 pounds. If the average weight of one person is 150 pounds, how many people can safely ride the elevator?
        a maximum of ___ people</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! 14</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.2. </para><para>The correct answer is 14. 
        An average person's weight is 150 pounds. The total amount of weight the elevator can hold is 2100 pounds.
        An average person's weight is 150 pounds. The total amount of weight the elevator can hold is 2100 pounds.
        Let \(p\) represent the number of people that can safely ride the elevator at one time.
        Let \(p\) represent the number of people that can safely ride the elevator at one time.
        The equation is: 150p≤2100
        Solve for \(p\): p≤14
        The number of people that can safely ride the elevator at one time is less than or equal to 14.
      
        The equation is: 150p≤2100
        Solve for \(p\): p≤14
        The number of people that can safely ride the elevator at one time is less than or equal to 14.
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_48ad04c3-a04c-4f81-b9f5-5e7b0acdff38" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>3. Jaden is looking at apartments with three of his friends. They want the monthly rent to be no more than $2360. If the roommates split the rent evenly among the four of them, what is the maximum rent each will pay?
        $____</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! 590</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.2. </para><para>The correct answer is 590. 
        There are 4 people that want to rent an apartment together. The total amount they want to pay for rent per month is $2360.
        There are 4 people that want to rent an apartment together. The total amount they want to pay for rent per month is $2360.
        Let \(r\) represent the amount of rent each person will pay.
        Let \(r\) represent the amount of rent each person will pay.
The equation is: 4r≤$2360 
The equation is: 4r≤$2360 
Solve for \(r\): r≤$590
Solve for \(r\): r≤$590
Each of the 4 people will pay a maximum of $590 per month for rent to stay under the total amount of $2360. 
Each of the 4 people will pay a maximum of $590 per month for rent to stay under the total amount of $2360. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_7d241ae8-5bad-435e-8aef-eb5e84781416" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>4. Melody got a $20 gift card for the coffee shop. Her favorite iced drink costs $3.79. What is the maximum number of drinks she can buy with the gift card?
        ____ drinks</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! 5</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.2. </para><para>The correct answer is 5.</para><para>Each drink costs $3.79. The total amount she has to spend is $20.
        Let \(d\) represent the number of drinks that can be purchased using the gift card.
        Let \(d\) represent the number of drinks that can be purchased using the gift card.
        The equation is: $3.79d≤$20 
        The equation is: $3.79d≤$20 
        Solve for \(d\): d≤5.28
        Solve for \(d\): d≤5.28
        She can buy a maximum of 5 drinks, which would cost $3.79 x 5 = $18.95.  That would leave $20 - $18.95 = $1.05 on the gift card, which is not enough to buy a 6th drink.
        She can buy a maximum of 5 drinks, which would cost $3.79 x 5 = $18.95.  That would leave $20 - $18.95 = $1.05 on the gift card, which is not enough to buy a 6th drink.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_39730d15-37e4-4f6b-90f3-cd46b7260741" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>5. Teegan likes to play golf. He has budgeted $60 next month for the driving range. It costs him $10.55 for a bucket of balls each time he goes. What is the maximum number of times he can go to the driving range next month?
        ____ times</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! 5</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.2. </para><para>The correct answer is 5. </para><para>Each bucket of balls costs $10.55. His total budget for the month is $60.
        Let \(n\) represent the number of times he can visit the driving range without going over his budget.
        Let \(n\) represent the number of times he can visit the driving range without going over his budget.
        The equation is: $10.55n≤$60 
        The equation is: $10.55n≤$60 
        Solve for \(n\): n≤5.69
        Solve for \(n\): n≤5.69
        He can go to the range 5 times, which would cost $10.55 x 5 = $52.75. He would be under budget by $60 - $52.75 = $7.25, which is not enough money for a 6th trip to the range.
        He can go to the range 5 times, which would cost $10.55 x 5 = $52.75. He would be under budget by $60 - $52.75 = $7.25, which is not enough money for a 6th trip to the range.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_55b9d39b-3707-4035-b44f-34aee7584723" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>6. Ryan charges his neighbors $17.50 to wash their car. How many cars must he wash next summer if his goal is to earn at least $1500?

        ____ cars</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! 86</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.2. </para><para>The correct answer is 86.
      </para><para>He charges $17.50 for each wash. He wants to make at least $1500.
        Let \(c\) represent the number of cars he has to wash to make at least $1500.
        Let \(c\) represent the number of cars he has to wash to make at least $1500.
        The equation is: $17.50c≤$1500 
        The equation is: $17.50c≤$1500 
        Solve for \(c\): c≤85.71
        Solve for \(c\): c≤85.71
        He has to wash 86 cars to make at least $1500. If he washes 85 cars he will make 85($17.50)=$1487.50, which is lower than his goal. If he washes 86 cars he will make 86($17.50)=$1505, which is higher than his goal. 
        He has to wash 86 cars to make at least $1500. If he washes 85 cars he will make 85($17.50)=$1487.50, which is lower than his goal. If he washes 86 cars he will make 86($17.50)=$1505, which is higher than his goal. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_3973a4a2-3e42-4e3e-9bdb-7902568e1d43" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>7. Alejandro’s water bill is $24.80 per month plus $2.20 per ccf (hundred cubic feet) of water. What is the maximum number of ccf Alejandro can use if he wants his bill to be no more than $60?

        ____ ccf</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! 16</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.2. </para><para>The correct answer is 16. 
        He pays a flat monthly fee of $24.80 plus an additional cost of $2.20 per ccf that he uses each month. He wants his bill to be no more than $60.
        He pays a flat monthly fee of $24.80 plus an additional cost of $2.20 per ccf that he uses each month. He wants his bill to be no more than $60.
        Let \(w\) represent the amount of water he uses each month.
        Let \(w\) represent the amount of water he uses each month.
        The equation is: $24.80+$2.20w≤$60
        The equation is: $24.80+$2.20w≤$60
        Solve for \(w\): $2.20w≤35.20
        Solve for \(w\): $2.20w≤35.20
        Solve for \(w\): w≤16
        Solve for \(w\): w≤16
        He can use less than or equal to 16 ccf of water per month and keep his bill under $60.

      
        He can use less than or equal to 16 ccf of water per month and keep his bill under $60.

      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_5b8372d5-500a-451c-8414-3a6ed0920e4c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>8. Look at the inequality \(5m + 6 &lt; 14 − 2m\). Which of the following is NOT true?</para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>If \(m\) is a negative number, then \(5m + 6\) could be a positive or negative number.</para><para>If \(m\) is a negative number, then \(14 − 2m\) will always be positive.</para><para>If \(m\) is a positive number, then \(5m + 6\) will always be positive.</para><para>If \(m\) is a negative number, then \(14 − 2m\) will always be negative.</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! If m is a negative number, then \(14 − 2m\) will always be negative
      </para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.3
      </para><para>The correct answer is if \(m\) is a negative number, then \(14 − 2m\) will always be negative.
        Substitute in -1 and -2 for \(m\) to evaluate statement a.
        Substitute in -1 and -2 for \(m\) to evaluate statement a.
        \(5m+6\) 
        \(5m+6\) 
        \(5(-1)+6=-5+6=1\)
        \(5(-1)+6=-5+6=1\)
        \(5(-2)+6=-10+6=-5\)
        \(5(-2)+6=-10+6=-5\)
        Statement a is true because when \(m\) is -1, \(5m+6\) is positive, but any negative number smaller than -1, will make it negative, so \(5m+6\) could be a positive or negative number.
        Statement a is true because when \(m\) is -1, \(5m+6\) is positive, but any negative number smaller than -1, will make it negative, so \(5m+6\) could be a positive or negative number.
        
        
        Substitute in -1 for \(m\) to evaluate statement b.
        Substitute in -1 for \(m\) to evaluate statement b.
        \(14−2m\)
        \(14−2m\)
        \(14-2(-1)=14+2=16\)
        \(14-2(-1)=14+2=16\)
        Statement b is true because when \(m\) is -1, \(14−2m\) is positive, and any negative number would make the expression positive because subtracting a negative number always results in a positive number. 
        Statement b is true because when \(m\) is -1, \(14−2m\) is positive, and any negative number would make the expression positive because subtracting a negative number always results in a positive number. 

        Substitute in 1 for \(m\) to evaluate statement c.

        Substitute in 1 for \(m\) to evaluate statement c.
        \(5m+6 \)
        \(5m+6 \)
        \(5(1)+6=5+6=11\)
        \(5(1)+6=5+6=11\)
        Statement c is true. If \(m\) is a positive number, then any other positive number will make 5m+6 positive number.
        Statement c is true. If \(m\) is a positive number, then any other positive number will make 5m+6 positive number.
        
        
        Because statement b is true for If \(m\) is a negative number, then \(14−2m\) will always be positive, statement d has to be false because \(14−2m\) can not be a negative number when \(m\) is negative. 
        Because statement b is true for If \(m\) is a negative number, then \(14−2m\) will always be positive, statement d has to be false because \(14−2m\) can not be a negative number when \(m\) is negative. 
        
        
        Statement d is the false statement.

      
        Statement d is the false statement.

      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_4a39ec82-5ca4-4e83-98c4-ce7e20d81106" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>9. Solve the inequality: \(x + 8 &gt; –12\)</para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x &gt; -20\)</para><para>\(x &gt; 20\)</para><para>\(x &gt; -4\)</para><para>\(x &gt; 4\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! x &gt; -20</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.5. </para><para>The correct answer is \(x &gt; -20\). 
        \(x+8\gt–12\)
        \(x+8\gt–12\)
        \(x+8-8\gt–12-8\)
        \(x+8-8\gt–12-8\)
        \(x\gt–20\)
      
        \(x\gt–20\)
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_151484b4-33c9-47f1-bba8-a13ea0c06711" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>10. Solve the inequality: \(x – 15 &lt;–3\)</para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x &lt; 18\)</para><para>\(x &lt; –12\)</para><para>\(x &lt; 12\)</para><para>\(x &lt; –18\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x &lt; 12\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.5. </para><para>The correct answer is \(x &lt; 12\). 
        \(x – 15 \lt–3\) 
        \(x – 15 \lt–3\) 
        \(x – 15+15 \lt–3+15\) 
        \(x – 15+15 \lt–3+15\) 
        \(x\lt12\)
      
        \(x\lt12\)
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_25e20b7e-f2e0-4d27-a5f8-83e5897771b6" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>11. Solve the inequality: \(x\;+\;19\;\leq\;23\)</para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x\;\geq\;4\)</para><para>\(x\;\leq\;-4\)</para><para>\(x\;\leq\;42\)</para><para>\(x\;\leq\;4\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x\;\leq\;4\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.5. </para><para>The correct answer is \(x\;\leq\;4\). 
        \(x + 19\leq 23\)
        \(x + 19\leq 23\)
        \(x + 19-19\leq 23-19\)
        \(x + 19-19\leq 23-19\)
        \(x\le24\)
      
        \(x\le24\)
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_0792ba8c-1bf4-4a75-8a9d-8685b9f5df6e" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>12. Solve the inequality: \(7x &lt; –56\)</para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x &gt; -8\)</para><para>\(x &lt; -8\)</para><para>\(x &lt; -63\)</para><para>\(x &lt; -49\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x &lt; -8\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.5 </para><para>The correct answer is \(x &lt; -8\). 
        \(7x \lt –56\)
        \(7x \lt –56\)
        \(\frac{7x}{7} \lt \frac{-56}{7}\)
        \(\frac{7x}{7} \lt \frac{-56}{7}\)
        \(x-\lt8\)
        \(x-\lt8\)
      
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_35a543cc-10ae-41fc-b50a-53ab41301600" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>13. Solve the inequality: \(\frac x2\;\;\leq\;\frac52\)</para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x\;\leq\;\frac52\)</para><para>\(x\;\leq\;5\)</para><para>\(x\;\leq\;2\)</para><para>\(x\;\geq\;5\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x\;\;\leq\;5\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.5 </para><para>The correct answer is \(x\;\;\leq\;5\). 
        \(\frac{x}{2}\leq\frac{5}{2}\)
        \(\frac{x}{2}\leq\frac{5}{2}\)
        \((2)(\frac{x}{2})\leq(2)(\frac{5}{2})\)
        \((2)(\frac{x}{2})\leq(2)(\frac{5}{2})\)
        \(x\leq5\)

      
        \(x\leq5\)

      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="a721f6ea-12af-448e-9c3e-c8116641b0e6" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>14. Solve the inequality: \(-3x &gt; 21\)</para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x &lt; 18\)</para><para>\(x &lt; -24\)</para><para>\(x &gt; -7\)</para><para>\(x &lt; -7\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x &lt; -7\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.5 </para><para>The correct answer is \(x &lt; -7\). 
        \(-3x\gt21\)
        \(-3x\gt21\)
        \(\frac{-3x}{-3}\gt\frac{21}{-3}\)
        \(\frac{-3x}{-3}\gt\frac{21}{-3}\)
        When you divide by a negative number, you need to switch the sign to make the statement true
        When you divide by a negative number, you need to switch the sign to make the statement true
        \(\frac{-3x}{-3}\lt\frac{21}{-3}\)
        \(\frac{-3x}{-3}\lt\frac{21}{-3}\)
        \(x\lt-7\)

      
        \(x\lt-7\)

      </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
